Dne četrtek, 09. julij 2015 12.52.39 UTC+2 je oseba Terrence Jorgensen napisala:

> > Would you suggest in that type of model reporting DWLS indices or robust versions (or both)?
>
> Robust shows better performance in simulation studies:
>
> http://dx.doi.org/10.1111/j.2044-8317.2012.02049.x
>
> > Since you an expert in SEM, can I kindly ask you for your (personal) opinion. Since reviewer explicitly suggested using ‘fiml’ method how to justify using ‘pairwise’?
>
> It is a question of availability.  Maximum Likelihood estimation in SEM software packages is always maximum-multivariate-normal likelihood.  Binary data are not multivariate-normally distributed, so you cannot use maximum (normal) likelihood -- FIML or otherwise.
>
> http://dx.doi.org/10.1037/a0029315
>
> > Or it would be better to impute (only) once missing values with e.g. k-nearest neighbours algorithm and run sem on that imputed dataset?
>
> Why only 1 imputation, and why that particular algorithm?  You should use multiple imputation, which is a preferred modern method for dealing with missing data because it only assumes MAR data, whereas pairwise deletion assumes MCAR.  This article provides conceptual introductions to MAR and MCAR assumptions, as well as FIML and multiple imputation, and includes some R syntax for an applied example:
>
> http://dx.doi.org/10.1093/jpepsy/jst048
>
> To use multiple imputation with lavaan, look into the R package "semTools" -- it includes a function runMI() that automates the process of (1) imputing missing data multiple times (using the "mice" or "Amelia" packages in R), (2) running your SEM on all imputed data sets using lavaan, and (3) combining the results.
>
> Terry
>

