On 07/17/2015 01:23 PM, Terrence Jorgensen wrote:
>
>     *model <- "*
>     *R=~a1*V+b1*F*
>     *C~a2*R*
>     *A ~ a3*R*
>     *
>     *
>     *P ~ a4*R+a5*A*
>     *C ~ a6*A+a7*P*
>     *"*
>     *fit <- sem(model, data = nivsem,ordered = c("V","F"),std.lv <http://std.lv>
>     <http://std.lv> = TRUE)*

>
>
> This model is still not identified.

I think the model is identified in theory. But we are unlucky with the
sample.

I tried simulating data with a large sample size, and it runs just fine:

set.seed(12345)
pop.model <- '
  R =~ 1*V + 0.6*F
  V | -0.5*t1
  F | +0.5*t1
  C ~ 0.5*R
  A ~ 0.7*R
  P ~ 0.6*R+0.9**A
  C ~ 0.2*A+0.3*P
'
Data <- simulateData(pop.model, sample.nobs = 10000)

model <- '
   R =~ a1*V + b1*F
   C ~ a2*R

   A ~ a3*R

   P ~ a4*R+a5*A
   C ~ a6*A+a7*P
'

fit <- sem(model, data = Data, ordered = c("V","F"), std.lv <http://std.lv> = TRUE)

Since we only have N=40, I would suggest avoiding the latent variable
'R' altogether, and construct some sort of an observed variable based on
V and/or F.

Yves.

